[Event "GBR-ch 46th"]
[Site "York"]
[Date "1959.08.10"]
[Round "1.17"]
[White "Barden, Leonard William"]
[Black "Clough, Fred"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "D28"]
[Annotator "Wood, BH"]
[PlyCount "162"]
[EventDate "1959.08.10"]
[EventType "swiss"]
[EventRounds "11"]
[EventCountry "GBR"]

{It may sound extravagant but I am prepared to maintain that nobody ever had
such a first week of vicissitudes in the British Championship as Leonard
Barden this year. By the week-end, he must have been feeling rather
punch-drunk. Let the games speak for themselves. (BH Wood, ILN)} 1. d4 d5 2. c4
dxc4 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 e6 5. Bxc4 c5 6. O-O a6 7. Nc3 b5 8. Bb3 Bb7 9. Qe2 Nc6
10. Rd1 Qc7 11. d5 exd5 12. e4 d4 13. e5 O-O-O 14. exf6 dxc3 15. a4 Rxd1+ 16.
Bxd1 gxf6 17. axb5 axb5 18. Qxb5 Bd6 19. bxc3 Rg8 20. Qb1 Qd7 21. Bc2 Ne7 22.
Be4 Rxg2+ 23. Kf1 Bxe4 24. Qxe4 Rg4 25. Ra8+ Bb8 26. Qe2 Qb7 27. Ra6 Re4 28.
Qd3 f5 {At this stage, Barden had left himself with barely four minutes for
his next twelve moves. Clough had about half an hour. As so often happens, it
was Clough whose nerves were the more severely affected. His 33rd move, which
is an outright blunder, lost him twenty minutes of his time. Recovering from
the shock in praiseworthy fashion, he manages to con­jure up a simple trap
into which Barden, now com­pelled to make every move on the instant, tumbles
headlong.} 29. Rf6 c4 30. Qc2 Qd5 31. Nd4 Bxh2 32. Be3 Qe5 33. Rh6 Rg4 $4 34.
Nf3 Qd5 35. Nxh2 Qh1+ 36. Ke2 Re4 37. Nf3 $4 {(In time trouble)} Qxh6 38. Qxe4
fxe4 39. Bxh6 exf3+ 40. Kxf3 Nf5 $2 {Phew! Time control at last. Clough has a
won endgame and more expert readers may enjoy them­selves unearthing a few of
the many winning con­tinuations he missed. Barden, on the other hand,
provides us with the demonstration (for which we have waited rather long!) of
the contrast in tech­nique between a man playing in his 10th British
Championship and one playing in his first.} 41. Bf4 Kd7 42. Kg4 Ke6 43. Kg5 f6+
44. Kg4 Nd6 45. f3 Nf7 46. Be3 Ne5+ 47. Kf4 h5 48. Ke4 h4 49. Bf4 h3 50. Bh2
f5+ 51. Kf4 Nd3+ 52. Ke3 Kd5 53. Ke2 f4 54. Kf1 Ke5 55. Bg1 Kf5 56. Bh2 Ne5 57.
Ke2 Nd3 58. Kf1 Ke5 59. Bg1 Kd5 60. Ba7 $2 {For instance, have a look at 60...
Ne1 now. The disappearance of one pawn on each side now raises Barden’s
drawing hopes considerably.} h2 61. Kg2 Ne1+ 62. Kxh2 Nxf3+ 63. Kg2 Ke4 64. Kf2
Ne5 65. Bd4 Ng4+ 66. Kg2 f3+ 67. Kg3 Ne3 $2 (67... f2 68. Bxf2 Nxf2 69. Kxf2
Kd3 $19) 68. Kf2 Nd1+ 69. Ke1 Nb2 70. Kf2 Nd3+ 71. Kg3 Nc1 72. Kf2 Nb3 73. Bg7
Nd2 74. Bd4 Kd3 75. Bg7 Kc2 $2 76. Ke3 Kd1 77. Kf2 Kc2 78. Ke3 f2 79. Kxf2 Ne4+
80. Ke1 Nxc3 81. Be5 Kd3 {Here a draw was agreed; if Black advances his pawn,
White sacrifices his bishop for it and White’s lone knight can never give
mate (even two can’t). Dear me! Time up. We shall have to defer the next
episode in our tale of wild time-scrambles, won games lost and lost games won,
until next week. (Source: Illustrated London News, 29 August 1959)} 1/2-1/2

