[Event "GBR-ch 46th"]
[Site "York"]
[Date "1959.08.13"]
[Round "4.3"]
[White "Hallmark, Alfred Maurice"]
[Black "Barden, Leonard William"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "E69"]
[Annotator "Wood, BH"]
[PlyCount "85"]
[EventDate "1959.08.10"]
[EventType "swiss"]
[EventRounds "11"]
[EventCountry "GBR"]

{Having brilliantly beaten Jonathan Penrose in round three of the British
Championship at York, Barden drew for his next opponent another who was almost
a newcomer, A. M. Hallmark, of Harrogate, had managed to qualify just once
before. So (naturally, in his form of this tournament!) Barden got into a
horrid mess:} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. g3 e5 7. Bg2
Nbd7 8. O-O c6 9. h3 Qa5 10. Qc2 a6 11. Rd1 b5 12. cxb5 cxb5 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14.
Nd4 Bb7 15. f4 b4 16. Na4 Rac8 17. Qb3 Nc4 18. Bf1 d5 19. e5 Ne4 20. a3 g5 21.
axb4 Qd8 22. Nc5 Nxc5 23. bxc5 gxf4 {Faced with the loss of a second pawn (at
least) if he retires the attacked bishop, Barden sacrifices desperately. He
now had about three minutes, Hall­mark about ten, for the next seventeen
moves.} 24. Qxb7 fxg3 25. Nf3 Nxe5 26. Qxd5 Qxd5 27. Rxd5 Nxf3+ 28. Kg2 Ne5 29.
Rxa6 Rfd8 30. Rxd8+ Rxd8 31. b4 h5 32. b5 h4 33. c6 Rc8 34. Bg5 Ng6 35. Ra4 Be5
36. Rc4 Bc7 37. Be3 Rb8 38. Rb4 Ne5 39. Rxh4 Kg7 40. Ra4 Kf6 {Of course, all
the moves since our last note were made in, probably, less time than you have
taken to make them simply reading from the score! It is therefore rather
pusillanimous to point out that, in trying to save his hazardously advanced
king’s knight’s pawn by playing 32...h4, Barden only flung good money
after bad.} 41. Ra7 Bb6 42. Bxb6 Rxb6 43. c7 {Source: Illustrated London News,
12 September 1959 - BH Wood's notes} 1-0

