[White "Barden, Leonard William"]
[Black "Wood, Baruch Harold"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "E85"]
[Annotator "Wood, BH"]
[PlyCount "107"]
[EventDate "1959.08.10"]
[EventType "swiss"]
[EventRounds "11"]
[EventCountry "GBR"]

{The opening of my own game against Leonard Barden in the British Championship
at York one of those happy-go-lucky affairs where the players attack on
opposite wings.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 e5 6. Nge2 Nc6 7.
Be3 O-O 8. d5 Ne7 9. Nc1 Ne8 10. b4 f5 11. Be2 Nf6 12. O-O f4 13. Bf2 g5 14. c5
Ng6 15. Nb3 h5 {If anybody is inquisitive about what really goes on inside
that British Championship arena, I will reveal that the game was summed up
here by the Universities’ International R. B. Edwards on the next board in a
sotto voce comment, "Yoicks! Tally ho." It is a pell-mell race of attacks on
opposite wings, in which every move counts.} 16. Rc1 Rf7 17. Na5 g4 18. Nb5 g3
19. hxg3 fxg3 20. Bxg3 h4 21. Bh2 Bh6 {The king’s side has got there first
and Barden is in a compromised situation. If the attacked rook moves 
(naturally to c3 to forestall ...Be3+) there comes ...Nh5, ...Ngf4, ...Rg7, ...
Qg5, etc, and White’s king succumbs to an annihilating onslaught. Friends
who freely advise me on how to win the British Championship might well ponder
on the course of this game and revise their estimates of the difficulty of the
process. It is not enough to accom­plish the feat of out-playing Leonard
Barden, one of the world's leading theorists, in the opening—and as Black;
you must follow-up with an impeccable middle-game and end game. For,
perceiving his peril in good time, he lashes out with calculated desperation.}
22. f4 Nxf4 23. Bxf4 Bxf4 24. Rxf4 exf4 25. e5 dxe5 26. Bc4 Bg4 (26... a6 $17 {
Houdini 4 Pro} 27. Nxc7 Qxc7 28. d6 Qd7) (26... Rg7 $1 $19 {Houdini 4 Pro}) 27.
Qe1 $11 {(Houdini 4 Pro)} Nxd5 28. Nxb7 Qd7 29. Qxe5 Rf5 ({A crucial stage.
The win must be there ; perhaps some reader can find it? I rejected} 29... c6 {
fearing} 30. N5d6 {Perhaps 29...Be6...? Now the tide turns.}) 30. Bxd5+ Qxd5
31. Qxd5+ Rxd5 32. Nxc7 Rd7 33. Nxa8 Rxb7 34. Rc4 Rf7 35. c6 h3 36. gxh3 ({
Now I am fighting to draw. Now if} 36. c7 {, then} Bc8 {and the knight is dead.
There is a maze of possibilities-in White’s favour-if White is
over-amhitious with 36.g3 Re7!}) 36... Bxh3 37. c7 Rf8 38. Rd4 Bc8 39. Rd8 Kf7
40. b5 Ke7 41. Rxf8 {(sealed)} Kxf8 42. a4 Ke7 43. a5 Kd6 44. a6 Kd7 (44...
Bxa6 45. bxa6 Kd7 46. Kg2 Kc8 47. Kf3 Kd7 48. Kxf4 Kc8 49. Ke5 Kd7 $11) ({Not} 
44... Kc5 {as I had planned, because of} 45. b6 $1 {which wins. Work it out!
It would take ten articles to do justice to this fantastic game. Let me only
confess that I played ten more moves and finally resigned, oblivious
throughout to the fact that ...Bxa6!!, bxa6, ...Ke7 would have secured a draw
because Black can oscillate his king between c8 and d7, any attempt by
White’s king to infiltrate resulting in stalemate.}) 45. Kf2 Kd6 46. Kf3 Ke5
$4 (46... Kc5 $11 47. b6 axb6 48. a7 Kd6) 47. Kf2 $4 (47. Nb6 $1 axb6 48. a7
$18) 47... Kd6 48. Kf3 Kd7 49. Kxf4 Kd6 50. Ke3 Kd7 51. Ke4 Kd6 52. Kd4 Bh3 $4
(52... Bxa6 $3 53. bxa6 Kd7 54. Ke5 Kc8 $11) 53. b6 axb6 54. a7 {Sources:
Illustrated London News, 19 September 1959; (moves 41-end) BCM, Oct 1959, p290}
1-0

