{"The biggest surprise of the round was Alexander’s loss to the Manchester
player Hilton. Alexander won a pawn with his favourite Ruy Lopez, but
underestimated the resulting counter­attack and Hilton’s pieces combined in
a crushing onslaught on the king." (The Guardian, 18 Aug 1959)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3
Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. Qe2 b5 6. Bb3 Bc5 7. a4 Rb8 8. axb5 axb5 9. d3 d6
10. O-O Bg4 11. c3 O-O 12. Be3 Qe7 13. Ra6 Nd8 14. Nbd2 Ne6 15. h3 Bh5 16. g4
Bg6 17. Bxe6 Bxe3 18. Bxf7+ Rxf7 19. fxe3 Nd7 20. b4 c5 21. Qe1 cxb4 22. cxb4
d5 23. Qb1 dxe4 24. dxe4 Rf6 25. Rxf6 Nxf6 26. Rc1 Bf7 27. Qb2 Nd7 28. Rc7 Qd6
29. Qc3 Bc4 30. Ra7 Rc8 31. Ra1 h5 32. gxh5 Qh6 33. Ra7 Nf6 34. Kf2 Qxh5 35. h4
Rd8 36. Ke1 Nxe4 37. Nxe4 Qxf3 38. Rxg7+ Kh8 39. Rh7+ Kg8 40. Rg7+ Kxg7 41.
Qxe5+ Kg8 42. Nf6+ Kf7 43. Qc7+ Ke6 {"This game ended with a curious incident.
Alexander, as a final trap, checked with his queen on a square where
Hilton’s queen could capture the checking piece for nothing. Hilton,
absorbed in looking for the way of manoeuvring his king to safety, thought for
a good five minutes before noticing the obvious winning move." (The Guardian,
18 Aug 1959)} 44. Qc6+ Qxc6 0-1

